@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In AGI, variable names use @code{lower_snake_case} (e.g., @code{player_score}, @code{current_room}).
Function names use @code{dotted.snake.case} (e.g., @code{object.load}, @code{player.move}).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in uppercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: IF, ELSE, THEN, GOSUB, GOTO, NEWROOM, LOADLOGICS, RETURN, QUIT, ASSIGN, INCREMENT, DECREMENT, SET, RESET, SAID, TEST, POSN, CONTROLLER, HAVEKEY
@item Operators: =, <, >, <=, >=, <> (for TEST command), +, - (for ASSIGN/INCREMENT/DECREMENT)
@item String delimiters: Double quotes (@code{"..."})
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

An AGI program consists of a series of commands executed sequentially unless altered by control flow statements.
Each line contains a single command. There is no explicit program header or termination statement.

@example
LOADLOGICS 1
NEWROOM 2
SET player_health 100
IF said("look") THEN
    PRINT "You see a sword on the ground."
ELSE
    PRINT "I don't understand."
END-IF
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

AGI does not have traditional object methods. Instead, objects are manipulated through commands that
reference object IDs directly. Object-specific behavior is implemented in logic files that respond
to events affecting particular objects.

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a routine that takes no arguments, simply use the command name:

@example
LOADLOGICS 3    @r{Load logic number 3}
RETURN          @r{Return from current logic}
QUIT            @r{Quit the game}
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

Some AGI commands accept a single value parameter that is conceptually passed in an accumulator:

@example
NEWROOM 5       @r{Change to room number 5}
SET score 100   @r{Set the score variable to 100}
INCREMENT lives @r{Increase lives counter by 1}
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

AGI does not have routines that return values in an accumulator in the traditional sense.
Instead, values are stored in variables and retrieved as needed.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

Not applicable in AGI's command structure.

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

AGI accesses library routines through built-in commands. All library calls are considered far
calls to the interpreter's built-in functionality:

@example
SAID "open door"    @r{Library call: check if player typed "open door"}
CONTROLLER 1        @r{Library call: read joystick position}
HAVEKEY "skeleton"  @r{Library call: check inventory for skeleton key}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for library calls in AGI
@item Far: All built-in commands are far calls to interpreter ROM
@item Library: Not applicable (built-ins are the library)
@item Object: Not applicable for interpreter built-ins
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

AGI does not support explicit bank switching in its standard command set.
All interpreter services are accessed through built-in commands that
implicitly handle any required bank switching.

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object manipulation in AGI is performed through specific commands rather
than method invocation:

@example
POSN ego 100 150 120 180   @r{Set object "ego" position rectangle}
SET ego.x 100              @r{Set object's x coordinate}
SET ego.y 150              @r{Set object's y coordinate}
@end example

These operations correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via SET command
@item Far: Not applicable
@item Library: POSN, SET commands access object engine
@item Object: Setting slots on object instances
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using the ASSIGN, SET, INCREMENT, or DECREMENT commands:

@example
ASSIGN player_score 0          @r{Set variable to value}
SET player_lives 3             @r{Alternative assignment syntax}
INCREMENT player_score         @r{Add 1 to variable}
DECREMENT player_lives         @r{Subtract 1 from variable}
END-IF                         @r{End conditional block}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots (properties) are assigned using the SET command with dotted notation:

@example
SET ego.x 160                @r{Set ego object's x coordinate}
SET ego.y 100                @r{Set ego object's y coordinate}
SET ego.view 5               @r{Set ego object's view number}
SET ego.loop 0               @r{Set ego object's loop number}
SET ego.cel 0                @r{Set ego object's cel number}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

AGI does not have native array syntax. Array-like behavior must be
implemented using individual variables or through interpreter-specific
mechanisms not available in standard AGI.

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic is performed using INCREMENT and DECREMENT (for +/- 1)
or the SET command with expressions:

@example
INCREMENT counter              @r{counter = counter + 1}
DECREMENT counter              @r{counter = counter - 1}
SET score (score + 10)         @r{score = score + 10}
SET diff (num1 - num2)         @r{diff = num1 - num2}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

AGI does not provide direct bit-shift operators. Multiplication and
division by powers of two must be implemented through repeated addition
or subtraction, or through lookup tables.

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions can be built by combining operations:

@example
SET total ((score * 10) + bonus)
SET average ((total / count) - penalty)
@end example

Note: Multiplication and division require library routines or
repeated addition/subtraction implementations.

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions are used primarily in IF and TEST commands:

@example
IF (score > 1000) THEN ...     @r{Greater than}
IF (score < 50) THEN ...       @r{Less than}
IF (score = 100) THEN ...      @r{Equality}
IF (score <> 0) THEN ...       @r{Inequality (not equal)}
IF (have.key "sword") THEN ... @r{Boolean function result}
END-IF                         @r{End conditional}
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses IF/THEN/ELSE/END-IF blocks:

@example
IF (player.x > 100) AND (player.x < 200) THEN
    SET player.direction RIGHT
ELSE
    SET player.direction LEFT
END-IF

IF said("look") THEN
    IF have.key "gold" THEN
        PRINT "You see the glittering gold key."
    ELSE
        PRINT "You see an old metal key."
    END-IF
ELSE
    PRINT "I don't understand that command."
END-IF
@end example

AGI does not have an EXAMINE/COND structure; complex conditions
are built using AND/OR combinations within IF statements.

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using GOTO statements with conditional tests:

@example
SET i 0
:loop
    DISPLAY counters i
    INCREMENT i
    IF (i < 10) THEN GOTO loop END-IF
    PRINT "Loop finished"
@end example

WHILE-style loops:
@example
SET lives 3
:game_loop
    IF (lives = 0) THEN GOTO game_over END-IF
    @r{Game logic here}
    GOTO game_loop
:game_over
    PRINT "Game Over"
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved through conditional GOTO statements:

@example
SET i 0
:process_items
    IF (i >= item_count) THEN GOTO done_processing END-IF
    IF (items i = ITEM_STOP) THEN GOTO skip_remaining END-IF
    @r{Process item i}
    INCREMENT i
    GOTO process_items
:skip_remaining
    PRINT "Skipped remaining items"
:done_processing
    PRINT "All items processed"
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

AGI does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own internal scripting system. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, AGI commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
@r{Original AGI code}
IF said("talk wizard") THEN
    LOADLOGICS 15
    NEWROOM 7
END-IF

@r{Desugared via semantic-sugar layer}
IF (said "talk wizard") THEN
    (:call-acc :target "TalkToWizard" :using nil)
END-IF
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through specific commands that
interact with the background picture resources:

@example
SET.TILE bg_tile 50 100     @r{Change tile at (50,100) to number bg_tile}
DRAW.PIC bg_picture         @r{Draw background picture number}
SHOW.PIC bg_picture         @r{Show previously drawn picture}
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (animated foreground elements) are manipulated using:

@example
ANIMATE.OBJ ego             @r{Start animation for ego object}
STOP.UPDATE ego             @r{Stop animation updates for ego object}
SET.VIEW ego 5              @r{Set ego's view to number 5}
SET.LOOP ego 0              @r{Set ego's loop to number 0}
SET.CEL ego 2               @r{Set ego's cel to number 2}
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation commands control positioning, visibility, and
interaction:

@example
POSN ego 100 100 150 150    @r{Set ego's movement boundaries}
SET.HORIZON 20              @r{Set vertical horizon line}
IGNORE.OBJ ego              @r{Make ego ignore other objects}
OBSERVE.OBJ ego             @r{Make ego interact with other objects}
DRAW ego                    @r{Draw ego object on screen}
ERASE ego                   @r{Remove ego object from screen}
@end example




@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features



The following AGI features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item Mouse control commands (MOUSE.*)
@item String concatenation and manipulation
@end table

@node BASIC
